   "subject": "Re: [methylkit_discussion] Question about differentially methylated region analysis.",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>Given the small differentially methylation difference between the treated and control group, the p and q value looks a little suspicious as I expect a little more variation between groups.</div><div> </div><div>My questions are:</div><div>(1) Is it normal to have a p and q value of zero? </div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>They are probably not exactly zero but very very small, but it gets output as 0.</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>(2) How is regional analysis calculated? My current understanding is that the counts for Cs (methylated) and Ts (unmethylated) are summed across regions and a logistic regression is performed between stress and control groups. Is my understanding of it correct?  </div><div> </div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Correct, if there are replicates. if not, it does fisher&#39;s exact test. you probably have lots of counts, that increases the power of detecting small differences. For simplicity, I will give an example of comparing frequencies with fisher&#39;s exact test, in this case having more counts gives you more confidence to discriminate small differences.</div><div>The following frequency tables represent numCs and numTs for a test and control case, the percent methylations are the same in both cases however the table with larger counts have a lower P-value.</div><div><br></div><div>fisher.test(matrix(c(10,10,15,10),nrow=2))<br></div><div>odds ratio=~ 0.67   P-value= 0.5572</div><div><br></div><div>fisher.test(matrix(c(100,100,150,100),nrow=2))<br></div><div>odds ratio=~ 0.67   P-value= 0.03611<br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div> </div></font></span></div><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;methylkit_discussion&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">methylkit_discus...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">methylkit_...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nVisit this group at <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/methylkit_discussion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/methylkit_discussion&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233972731000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0ddNeKgJn6wigFZ30XpI1J\">http://groups.google.com/group/methylkit_discussion</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233972731000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1gCr6X9E9u0d-u3WZYArYc\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div></div>\r\n"
